ick through the  papers  thinks what are they gonna what should  we do to sort of you know stay in the  story today  and he actually did that thing about  rubbing his fingers feeling the wind and  that is not government that is not  leadership and i think what cummings  exposed  is that if the country elects somebody  like johnson as prime minister  then the country is going to pay a very  heavy price for that we've paid it in  this pandemic  let's leave aside the question of  whether all journalists  behave in that way alastair i think i  know some who actually read  the newspapers and and so on but do you  i get your point about johnson sawn but  the truth is  it doesn't feel like the country really  agrees with you  even after cummings is uh testimony this  week opinion polls  have conservatives between 10 and 18  points  ahead and indeed in some polls they've  increased their lead post comings  well listen i honestly don't care about  the polls i care about  what i think about the country and where  the country is heading  with this prime minister and this  cabinet and you were talking there to  some of the principles of government  with lord kurzlake  well let me just go through the  principles that are meant to govern  standards in public life the nolan  principles honesty  openness which bob talked about  objectivity  selflessness integrity accountability  and leadership by example i think  johnson only scores on the last one  in that it leads by the example that  tells his cabinet ministers  they can be as useless as gavin  williamson they can break the  ministerial code as often as pretty  patel  they can lie in the way that cummings  says that the hancock has  and none of them will get held to  account for that because johnson doesn't  want to get help them held to account  because he's the worst of the lot when  it comes to dishonesty in public life so  the polls i think are a reflection of  all sorts of things their reflection  about the state of the opposition  they're a reflection of the success of  the vaccination program and people  feeling maybe maybe maybe we're just  coming out of this  this hell they're also a reflection of  the fact that i think the media  does not hold this government to account  nor does parliament frankly  does not hold this government to account  in the way that they should i think the  media has become incredibly supine in  its coverage of the government  and even on the coming stuff yes  wall-to-wall coverage  but then by the weekend it's oh well who  cares what dominic cummings thing the  sun's shining  vaccination programs going well  johnson's got married let's just move on  and i think the country's just got to  decide do we care  about the fact that we have a government  and a prime minister that was coming  and i agree with him this they are  directly responsible for tens of  thousands of deaths do we care about  that or not  do we care that a prime minister stands  up in parliament you've now got that  video from peter stevanovic  over 20 million views hardly anybody in  the mainstream media covering it  where they set out we talk about has he  broken the ministerial code it breaks  the ministerial code virtually every  time he stands up in parliament they're  meant to tell the truth  and this is what populism has done to  our politics  i we could talk about um your views uh  about uh this this government forever  and  you know and you know i you've been  pretty clear let's let's put it that way  on that but  let's just uh talk about some of the  underlying substantive questions here  some of the ones that i've talked  i was talking to bob kerslaker about  about the nature of government because  you've been  inside it one new labour minister  complained why i think while you were  still  there that he thought that when you got  into government  there was this great machine and you're  if you're a minister  you pull a lever and the machine wears  into life  and stuff happens and changes take place  and what he discovered within months was  that you pull a lever  and it wasn't connected to anything and  nothing  happened is that um sounded about right  to you  no it doesn't and also i think that's a  bit naive because if you think about how  we got into power  in 1997 was partly recognizing a lot of  the weaknesses in the system that if you  like we were up against and i think that  you know to be fair to the civil service  at the time yes there was some who were  obstructive yes there were some who were  difficult yes there were some who  weren't you know very good at the job  but a lot of them were and i think what  you've you know sorry to keep going back  to the current  government compared to a kind of what i  would call a rational government and a  sensible professional government  what happened was that when there was  clear direction from ministers  i think actually the civil service was  you know was broadly responsive  and i think one of the problems in the  pandemic has been the relentlessness of  the kind of mixed messaging  people not really knowing what they're  meant to be saying what they're meant to  be doing  johnson just making up on the hoof  whereas actually listen there were lots  of situations and tony blair famously  made that speech about the scars on his  back  about the difficulties of getting public  service reform  however we did deliver a lot of public  service reform  not least with the help of the best of  the best in the civil service but where  i go with bob  kersley by the way i do think we've got  to move away from all this language we  talk about the rolls-royce civil service  we have the best armed forces in the  world we have some of the best  but i think that we this british  exceptionalism is a is really not a  sensible way to kind of approach issues  like  the ones you're talking about let me  take it let me take you back to those  new labor years out of scandal um you  know you say you  they did t