nt  uh late february actually carry on as  normal there's no reason why we think  it's going to affect you  but we could see what was happening in  europe and we as a provider actually  locked down much earlier actually on the  11th of march because we could we could  see what was coming but reassuring staff  and relatives and visitors was very very  difficult because at that time  no one knew what was actually going to  be happening it was frightening  i want to um show you i don't know if  you'll be able to see it there's a  graph of the excess deaths  uh over the last year to february  in um uh care or  people who have been in chaos people  have been in care homes and  last april the peak climbed to  6 000. i mean normally it's you know  down  uh around a quarter or third or a  fifth of that um  tell me what do you felt during  that spike i mean it must have been  mayhem  well it was i mean we we had someone who  was transferred to us from hospital  actually on the 19th of march  um at that time we thought testing was  happening in fact we found it wasn't and  we actually had to go and get the test  ourselves from the hospital  to test that person to make sure that  they weren't um  uh positive by the time we got the test  we subsequently found they were positive  and they were  re-admitted back and unfortunately  passed away yes it was dreadful we were  worried because we saw the nhs  um was dealing with a very difficult  issue they had ppe  we didn't and was reassuring our staff  and our residents that we knew what was  happening and doing the best we possibly  could it was frightening um and if it  weren't for  the hard work of social care staff and  in fact the local authorities in our  area  they would those figures i'm i'm  confident would have been much much  worse  mr cummings said this week that um  when matt hancock had put said he'd put  a shield  around care homes he was telling  untruths um somebody  is not telling the truth here uh hancock  says  he